        <div id="vs-rivals" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Bloom vs. the Alternatives
          </h2>

          <p>
            Against <strong>Dymatize ISO100</strong>, Bloom loses on numbers. ISO100 lists about 25 g of protein per scoop from hydrolyzed whey isolate, Dymatize states it carries Informed Choice certification, and it is usually cheaper per 25 g, with a far wider flavor range. Bloom wins only on its specific flavors and branding. Our <a href="/dymatize-protein-powder-review/">Dymatize protein powder review</a> covers the ISO100 range in more detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            Against <strong>Myprotein Clear Whey Isolate</strong>, Bloom Clear Protein loses on protein quality. Myprotein's clear whey delivers about 20 g of protein from whey isolate alone, per its label, rather than splitting the dose with collagen. It is widely available, comes in many fruit and tea-style flavors, and is often discounted. If you want clear protein for muscle, it is the more efficient drink.
          </p>

          <p>
            Against flavor-focused brands, Bloom is closer to the pack. Brands such as Ghost and PEScience have also built followings on dessert and coffee flavors, and they typically match or beat Bloom's protein per scoop. Our <a href="/ghost-protein-powder-review/">Ghost protein powder review</a> is a good next read if branding and flavor are what draw you to Bloom in the first place.
          </p>

          <p>
            Where Bloom does have an edge is convenience for existing Bloom customers. If you already buy its greens or energy products direct, adding a tub of protein to the same order is easy, and the brand's flavors are designed to sit together. That is a legitimate reason to buy, as long as you know you are paying a little extra for it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Who Should Skip Bloom
          </h2>

          <p>
            Skip Bloom if you are a drug-tested athlete. With no NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport status advertised, there is no public database to confirm the product has been screened for banned substances. Choose a certified isolate instead and confirm the exact product and flavor in the certifier's database before you buy.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip it if you avoid artificial sweeteners. The Whey Isolate label we found uses sucralose and acesulfame potassium, and buyers who are sensitive to sweetness often say the flavors are too strong. A stevia-sweetened or unflavored isolate gives you more control over taste and fewer additives.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip Clear Protein as your main post-workout shake if muscle gain is your priority. With 5 g of its 20 g coming from collagen, the complete protein dose is about 15 g, which is light for most adults after training. Use it as a between-meal top-up instead, or choose a clear whey that is all isolate.
          </p>

          <p>
            And skip both if you have a milk allergy. Whey isolate is low in lactose but is still milk protein. Anyone with a diagnosed allergy, kidney disease, a pregnancy or a medical condition that affects protein intake should talk to a doctor or registered dietitian before using any protein powder. Nothing in this review is medical advice.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Reading 20 g as 20 g of whey</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Bloom Clear Protein lists 20 g of protein, but Bloom states 5 g of that comes from collagen peptides. Collagen lacks tryptophan and is low in leucine, so it does far less for muscle than whey. For training purposes, count each serving as roughly 15 g of whey isolate, not 20 g.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming a lifestyle brand is certified</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Bloom does not advertise NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport status on its protein. That is fine for most people, but drug-tested athletes need a certified product. Always confirm the exact product and flavor in the certifier's public database rather than trusting a brand's popularity or packaging.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Paying resale prices</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Bloom's protein is sold mainly direct and, for Clear Protein, at Target. Some marketplace listings have shown Bloom whey well above Bloom's own list price. Check the seller and compare with Bloom's site before buying, and use cost per 25 g of protein, not price per tub, to judge the deal.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the sweetener</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The Bloom whey flavor we checked is sweetened with sucralose and acesulfame potassium, and many buyers describe it as very sweet. If you dislike artificial sweeteners or find most protein too sweet, try a sample or a single tub first, or choose a stevia-sweetened or unflavored isolate instead.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a light drink as a full meal shake</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Clear Protein's 90 calories and roughly 15 g of whey make it a snack, not a meal replacement. Using it to replace a meal can leave you short on calories, fiber and protein. Pair it with food, or use a full-dose whey isolate shake after training.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

